  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract activity in Alaska through FY2026 to date shows $2.9B in obligations across 7,728 awards, with an average award size of $370K. Obligations are down 25.7% year over year, and current-year totals are partial since the fiscal year is still in progress. Construction-related work dominates the state: Commercial and Institutional Building Construction accounts for $935M of obligations. The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency at $1.1B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $552M and the General Services Administration at $483M. Large single vendors are concentrated in construction and services: DAVIS CONSTRUCTORS & ENGINEERS, INC. leads with $464M, while TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP accounts for $227M and TUTOR PERINI CORPORATION accounts for $225M. Other notable industry concentrations include Pharmacy Benefit Management at $227M, Facilities Support Services at $190M, and Security Guards and Patrol Services at $162M. Year-to-date patterns show heavy investment in building construction and defense buying, with several specialized vendors capturing large shares of obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove the year-over-year decline in obligations?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 25.7% year over year. The fiscal-year-to-date total is $2.9B, smaller than recent annual totals such as FY25 at $3.9B and FY24 at $3.8B. A shift in spending away from peak construction years helps explain part of the gap because Commercial and Institutional Building Construction was $935M but prior years show higher full-year totals. Large buyers like the Department of Defense remain active at $1.1B, but overall obligation volume is lower in the partial FY2026 period."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors control most federal buying in Alaska?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $1.1B in obligations, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $552M and the General Services Administration at $483M. On the vendor side, DAVIS CONSTRUCTORS & ENGINEERS, INC. received $464M, while TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP and TUTOR PERINI CORPORATION received $227M and $225M respectively. Those buyers and vendors, together with industry segments like building construction at $935M, account for a large share of FY2026 to date activity."
      }
    ]
  },
